WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.843
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.843 --> 00:00:13.042
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Marissa,

00:00:13.042 --> 00:00:14.834
it's such a pleasure to
have you here today.

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:15.668
&gt;&gt; Thank you for having me.

00:00:15.668 --> 00:00:16.742
&gt;&gt; Welcome back to Stanford.

00:00:16.742 --> 00:00:18.610
&gt;&gt; I'm very excited to be here.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.120
&gt;&gt; I'd love to start out with
your time at Stanford here.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:27.730
So, you came in pre-med with
ambitions to become a physician, and

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.160
you transitioned over to symbolic systems.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.690
What was that pivot like?

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.500
&gt;&gt; Well so, I was very precise in
what I thought I wanted to do.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:42.050
I had looked at a lot of schools before
picking Stanford and in those travels,

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.650
I had met this really fascinating woman,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.050
very famous pediatric heart
surgeon who works at Duke.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.048
And her name is Marjorie Tripp.

00:00:51.048 --> 00:00:55.995
And I talked to her, and she said,
I I'm a doctor, I do extraordinary cases,

00:00:55.995 --> 00:01:00.264
but I also teach at the med school and
I thought, that sounded so great.

00:01:00.264 --> 00:01:04.370
So, I realized that wasn't interested
in hearts, but I was like, I'll

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:08.727
be a pediatric neurosurgeon, I love kids,
and I'm very interested in the brain,

00:01:08.727 --> 00:01:11.490
how it develops, so
I came here very intent on that.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:17.220
And through my freshman year, the
chemistry course started getting going on,

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.280
the biology core, went home for
the summer after my freshman year and

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.900
kind of compared notes with all of my
different friends, I'm from Wisconsin,

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.020
who'd gone to different schools.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.480
And I realized that we were learning
very much the same things all over.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:37.460
And I thought, I'd had the opportunity to
go to University of Wisconsin Madison,

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:38.410
it's a great school.

00:01:39.458 --> 00:01:41.770
And, because of various scholarships and
things,

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.950
that would have been a nearly free option.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.227
Stanford, on the other hand,
is very much not free.,

00:01:47.227 --> 00:01:48.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.760
&gt;&gt; It's still that way.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:56.470
And I thought to myself, wait,
I'm taking my parents retirement accounts.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.250
We're investing a lot here, and

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.470
I should do something at Stanford
that's really unique to Stanford.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.994
And something that can be done
just especially here, and so

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:11.660
I started thinking about the fact
that I would have a lot of

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.210
time later in life to specialize, and
go on, probably do other degrees.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.960
And, so
I was thinking of what I wanted to do.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.322
And as I was flying back that fall
to come back to my sophomore year,

00:02:22.322 --> 00:02:24.425
I was going through the course catalog and

00:02:24.425 --> 00:02:28.090
I found this department called
Symbolic Systems and it had Philosophy,

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:31.720
Psychology, Linguistics and
Computer Science all wrapped into one.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.134
And I knew the staff was
very strong in psychology,

00:02:34.134 --> 00:02:35.773
very strong in computer science.

00:02:35.773 --> 00:02:38.993
I had taken CS 105A the previous year,

00:02:38.993 --> 00:02:44.881
which is funny because it's the computer
science course for non-majors.

00:02:44.881 --> 00:02:48.447
And the fact that the lecturer opens
the class the first day and says,

00:02:48.447 --> 00:02:51.215
studies have shown that exactly
two of you will go on to

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:53.883
do anything additional in
computer science ever.

00:02:53.883 --> 00:02:56.828
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In a sea of 400 people

00:02:56.828 --> 00:02:57.792
[CROSSTALK] yeah.

00:02:57.792 --> 00:03:02.842
And so, and I knew I liked computer
science though and I had done well in that

00:03:02.842 --> 00:03:09.280
class, and interested in philosophy but
didn't know a lot about linguistics.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:15.630
Came back, took the introductory syntax
course from Professor Tom Wassau.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:19.880
And when I saw him because he was also the
director of the Symbolic Systems program,

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.278
and I knew he would try and
sell me on changing my major.

00:03:22.278 --> 00:03:23.790
He was probably like, okay, look,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.850
there's a special program,
most colleges don't have Symbolic Systems.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:30.180
It combines some of the departments
whereas Stanford is really strong.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.600
It was something that
was of interest to me.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.880
And, the idea of Symbolic Systems is
really, how do people learn and think?

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.980
So, that interest I had in
neuroscience was carried over

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.560
because I realized I was less
interested in cutting up brains and

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.570
more interested in how do you learn,
how do you think?

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:47.850
because it's cognitive psychology.

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.160
How do people learn?

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.170
Logic, how do people reason?

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.680
Linguistics, how do people
express themselves?

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.140
And then computer science, can you
have a computer that does the same?

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.340
So AI was of a real interest.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.550
But anyway, I want to talk about
Sir Wassau, when I walked in and I said,

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.110
I'm thinking about Symbolic Systems,
I'm taking your intro to syntax course.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.500
And he said, [SOUND] you should absolutely
become a Symbolic Systems major.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:11.850
And I knew he would say that, but

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.402
then he kind of did this interesting kind
of reverse psychology twist where he said,

00:04:15.402 --> 00:04:17.620
you should absolutely be
a Symbolic Systems major.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.150
And I thought he would say because the
classes are so good, the lecturers are so

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:21.810
good, the professors are so good.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.530
But he said, you should absolutely
be a Symbolic Systems major.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.645
All the most interesting
Stanford students are.

00:04:26.645 --> 00:04:28.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it was almost like if you weren't,

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.892
you weren't interesting.

00:04:30.892 --> 00:04:37.760
But I have to say, it was one of the
things that I learned in that, and also

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.786
went on to learn it at Google and other
places at some of my internships, etc.

00:04:41.786 --> 00:04:45.330
Is the Symbolic Systems major,
that time and I believe it's true today,

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.890
it's obviously true here at GSB,
was just filled with really,

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.070
really interesting students.

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:54.380
And I learned as much from the people
around me as I did from my professors,

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.760
the people in my study groups, the people
I went on to do an honor's thesis,

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:00.650
there were five of us in that major
that year that did an honor's thesis,

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:01.510
we met every week.

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:07.250
And people were doing musical puppets,
and could you do Stereovision

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.260
with cheap web cameras, and they were
just doing really interesting things.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:14.210
And so, it was a terrific decision.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.250
I will say it was a little bit scary,
because at the same time,

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.100
people are like what is Symbolic Systems.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:18.830
And still to this day,

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.240
I have to tell everyone what a Symbolic
Systems is, why does it make sense?

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.120
My father really wanted to know,
paying the bills for Stanford,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.430
like, what is Symbolic Systems,
and what will you use it for?

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:33.370
But, it was an amazing major that really
let me experience the range of Stanford,

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.100
continue to go after my passions,

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.680
which really sort of center a lot
more around artificial intelligence.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.180
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:46.070
&gt;&gt; And it also set up really amazing
set of opportunities posting effort.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.930
&gt;&gt; That's great, so one of those
opportunities included joining

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:52.110
a 20 person startup,
which turned out to be the right choice.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.230
But at the time,
what was that decision like,

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.520
turning down offers to teach at Carnegie
Mellon or to go work for McKenzie?

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well so
at the time Google was just eight people.

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.160
It was the spring of 1999.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.945
It's funny, because I actually spent
the 97, 98 school year living in Schwab.

00:06:10.945 --> 00:06:13.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I wasn't a business school student,

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:18.550
but they had about 20 other students
who lived in the dorms for balance, so

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.475
I was one of the balance people in Schwab.

00:06:20.475 --> 00:06:24.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it was the first year it was open

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:26.480
and I would go down every night and
work out on the treadmill, and

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.010
there were all these GSB students who were
very curious about who is this person

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.090
who's not in the GSB who's on
the treadmill every night?

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.091
And they would come, and then when they
would find out I was computer science,

00:06:34.091 --> 00:06:37.515
they'd be like I've got a friend with
a startup or I've got a startup.

00:06:37.515 --> 00:06:40.858
I mean, so I would literally go
down to the Schwab treadmill and

00:06:40.858 --> 00:06:42.280
get job offers on the way.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.975
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, that was kind of the process and

00:06:45.975 --> 00:06:47.775
it was just that time.

00:06:47.775 --> 00:06:51.560
The Internet was very heady and so
it was very easy to pick up offers.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.156
In the end I picked up about 14 offers and
I had to sort of sort and

00:06:56.156 --> 00:07:00.344
shift through them all,
shift and sort through them all.

00:07:00.344 --> 00:07:07.040
And, there were a lot of different
things that weighed on me at the time.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.710
And, it was easy for me to pick
the best offer of each type, right?

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.440
I knew if I was going to do consulting,
I was most interested in Mackenzie.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:16.540
If I was going to do a big company,
I was most interested in Oracle.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.520
I've done a lot of lecturing here at
Stanford and I had an offer to go to

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:22.240
Carnegie Mellon and
lecture in their Computer Science program.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.600
And so, if I did that I would go there.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.650
If I did a startup,
it was probably going to be Google.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:29.860
But I found it very difficult to integrate
across the different disciplines because

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:32.520
the job offers were so different.

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:36.410
So, I went and I thought a lot about it,

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:41.240
I procrastinated a lot and I told myself
I had to make a decision on May 1st.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.543
And, I have a friend who now
works at Yahoo with me but

00:07:44.543 --> 00:07:49.215
was a friend of mine from undergrad and
did a joint JD MBA here at Stanford.

00:07:49.215 --> 00:07:51.876
A good friend of mine named Andre Veneer.

00:07:51.876 --> 00:07:54.560
One of my longest friends.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.090
And Andre is amazing right?

00:07:56.090 --> 00:08:00.630
He was like the econ poly sci like 4.3
grade point average, can analyze anything.

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:03.720
Crazy, was working at
McKinsey at the time and

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.780
I said okay, I just need somebody who
can help me really synthesize across all

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:09.930
these different types of jobs and
analyze it.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:15.966
And so we sat down and
we had this big matrix where we had.

00:08:15.966 --> 00:08:21.120
Location, salary, job trajectory,
happiness factor and

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.200
like I then remember there are even
things where we were like look,

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:27.020
we can't get these with the street values,
we need curves so we were like [LAUGH]

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:30.340
Plotting curves and these things like and
then we were like some of these even need

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:34.300
third dimension and
we were going crazy plotting all of this.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:38.260
And we got really into the analysis of
the problem, and at about midnight,

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:43.160
I just got really overwhelmed and sort
of sat back and just got very emotional.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:46.675
And I just didn't really notice because
he was deeply involved in graphing.

00:08:46.675 --> 00:08:48.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And also it turned to me, and

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.640
he was like, what are you so upset about?

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.400
And I was like I just don't know.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.220
I told myself I'd make
a job sitting around.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:56.100
I just don't know.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.630
There's still 14 choices on the table,
I just can't figure it out.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.250
And got really upset and overwhelmed.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:09.532
And Andre, I think not really
kind of quite where my head was at.

00:09:09.532 --> 00:09:11.740
I said, Marissa,
this has been really fun for me.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.470
[LAUGH] My daughter's like, really?

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.740
because it's not really fun for me.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:20.070
And I found it very stressful and
he was like look, it's been fun for me.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.060
You know I love graphing and
analyzing things.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.468
And he's like I think I now understand
this problem as well as you do.

00:09:25.468 --> 00:09:29.440
And he like, I have to tell you,
I think you're approaching it all wrong.

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:32.250
He's like, because I'm watching you, and
you are sitting here looking at these and

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:33.580
you're looking for the right choice.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:35.690
That's interesting that you said
in your question the right choice.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:39.740
And he's like you're sitting here and
you're looking for the right choice and

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:43.340
he's like, you think there's one
right choice and 13 wrong choices,

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:47.695
I guess I understand as well as you do and
that's just not what I see here at all.

00:09:47.695 --> 00:09:50.970
He's like I see 14 very good choices and
then there's the one

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:55.680
that you're going to pick and commit to
and may create and feel good about and

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:59.550
he's like and it's not going to be
a matter of right versus wrong.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:00.470
It's just like it's all good.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:02.790
And then there's the one that's great and
you commit to.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:05.850
And people have asked me what's the best
piece of advice I've ever gotten?

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.090
And it's easily,
it was that moment from Andre.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:09.460
And that, because he's like,

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:13.340
look, a lot of times, smart people when
they get really committed to a problem,

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.760
start to analyze it as if there's only one
great option and all the others are not.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:21.160
And that's just not how life works and
it's important to realize when you get

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.730
really overwhelmed in those kinds
of choices to think about it.

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.690
To really think about it that way.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.450
And so look, go to bed, sleep on it, and

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.310
whatever you think of first thing in
the morning that's what you should do.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.030
And he's like, there's going to be a ton
of reasons you can articulate why you're

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:38.490
picking that,
there's going to be some reasons that

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:42.100
you can't articulate why you're
picking that and follow your gut.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.390
Because you really have analyzed and
understood it.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:48.500
So I went to bed, woke up in the morning
and I wanted to work at Google.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:51.140
I wanted to work at Google because I felt
like the smartest people were there back

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:54.080
to Tom Mosso's comment about always work
and surround yourself with interesting and

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:55.670
smart people.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.830
I wanted to work at Google because
I felt really unprepared to do it,

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.230
like the name itself
could be a punch line.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:06.108
I could imagine future family reunions
like when Marissa graduated from Stanford

00:11:06.108 --> 00:11:08.644
she went to this company called,
get this, Google.

00:11:08.644 --> 00:11:12.406
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She majored in symbolic and

00:11:12.406 --> 00:11:13.570
then she went to Google, and.

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:18.670
It was very high risk, but I thought,
well, look, if there's ever a time when

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:24.880
I'm going to take that kind of risk,
it was then, it was not now.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:30.040
Professor Roberts was a long-time mentor
of mine in the computer science world.

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:32.590
And he said look,
this is a great moment to go to and

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.910
participate in an entrepreneurial culture,
in the startup culture.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.520
He's like no one's going to fault you, you
graduated with a computer science degree

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.120
in 1999, for going to a start up and
taking a risk.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.520
He's like you can always pursue
some of these other options later.

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:50.305
But that was I just think
very much the right choice.

00:11:50.305 --> 00:11:53.100
For me it was about the smartest
people being there and

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.150
doing something I didn't feel ready to do.

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.950
When I thought about how to
make the choice, those were,

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.980
I had all the different things in the
matrix, but ultimately, I thought about

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:04.820
some of the best choices I had made, and
almost all the best choices I had made.

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:07.700
Choosing to come to Stanford,
changing my major,

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:12.090
different internships I gone to: they were
all very different types of decisions, but

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:14.990
the two things where I always looked for
the smartest people I could find, and

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.710
I always did something I felt
a little unprepared to do.

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:19.190
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:22.450
So now its come full circle because
you're former mentor at Google,

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:25.500
Eric Schmitz teaches of
course here at GSB and

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:29.040
I have the privilege of speaking
to him this morning about you and

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.990
he had to say about the associate particle
energy program that you started at Google,

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:39.930
that it was quote influential in
changing the industry forever.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:44.320
It produce all of our best leaders and
many leaders at startups around us.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:48.000
So given that, what is it that you are
most proud of from your time at Google?

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.880
&gt;&gt; I mean I'm very proud of the associate
product management program.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:54.180
I did run it for ten years.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:58.070
We hired on average about 20 people
a year, sometimes larger classes, so

00:12:58.070 --> 00:13:00.440
about 250, I call them my kids
because they are my kids.

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:05.020
Like I go to their weddings, I buy baby
presents and presents for their kids.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.408
It's been amazing to watch
them all grow up, and

00:13:09.408 --> 00:13:14.274
they have populated the leadership
positions, and basically Airbnb, Dropbox,

00:13:14.274 --> 00:13:18.375
Uber, Facebook, Twitter,
have all taken leaders on that program,

00:13:18.375 --> 00:13:22.358
including a lot of the leaders today
at Google coming through that.

00:13:22.358 --> 00:13:25.037
So I'm very proud of that, but

00:13:25.037 --> 00:13:31.017
I think it's hard to beat the feeling
that we created a technology and

00:13:31.017 --> 00:13:36.070
a product and
a business that changed the world.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:39.050
When you look at Google, and
it was such a privilege and

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:43.420
an honor to be part of a team and
play a role in that.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:46.570
I still remember I started
at Google in June, and

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:50.470
that December, the start up lifestyle,
you don't get a lot of time off.

00:13:50.470 --> 00:13:54.660
And I managed that December to get away,
and go to Switzerland,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:56.934
and I walked into a cyber-cafe.

00:13:56.934 --> 00:13:59.177
They still had those then.

00:13:59.177 --> 00:14:01.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:03.370
there was somebody using
Google on the screen.

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.310
The same way I'd remember sometimes
when I was backpacking around

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.290
Europe after I graduated
from undergraduate,

00:14:08.290 --> 00:14:13.720
I saw a Stanford sweatshirt on
the streets of, I think it was Florence.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:16.830
And you see so many of them in your
everyday life here at Stanford that you

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:17.650
didn't even think about it.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.880
I was like wait,
that's a Stanford sweatshirt,

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:21.040
what's it doing here in Florence?

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.060
It must be a Stanford student.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:26.470
Same thing, like I had gotten so
used to seeing the Google homepage and

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:28.220
the Google results page,
I didn't even think about it.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:30.640
I remember walking in to that cyber cafe,
seeing it, and

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.460
then all of a sudden being like
wait like I'm in Switzerland.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:34.840
They're using Google in Switzerland.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:37.490
There's this little project that
there's like 20 of us working

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:40.460
on on the other side of the world and
it's made it's way all the way here and

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:41.680
there's somebody using it.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:47.190
And it really showed me in that moment how
fast the Internet allows technology to

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:52.060
move and how quickly you can deploy
something and really get it to scale and

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:56.790
to a global impact which is when you think
about what the internet has done, and

00:14:56.790 --> 00:15:00.040
what type of impact it's enabled,
it's really amazing.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:03.520
But that feeling I had, that December,
seeing that search screen

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:08.210
on that cyber cafe in Switzerland,
I still have today.

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:12.390
I still am very proud of the impact
that Google has had overall, and

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:17.510
I think at both Google and Yahoo,
I had to write a post this past

00:15:17.510 --> 00:15:22.110
summer when we sold Yahoo to Verizon about
the company and there's so many things

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.580
that make Yahoo amazing, there's so
many things that make Google amazing.

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:26.740
But for me,

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:31.660
it's just a real privilege to work at
a company that's changed the world.

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.790
And you can make that argument for
both Google and

00:15:34.790 --> 00:15:39.260
Yahoo very easily and
that's really what I'm most proud of.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:42.470
&gt;&gt; Okay.
You've mentioned earlier that part of

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:46.400
the reason that you knew that Google
is the right decision that you made.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:49.930
That you decided to make right,
is that you didn't feel prepared.

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:54.680
So, stepping into Yahoo as
the fourth CEO in 12 months,

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:59.220
turning the company around from a medium
business into a products company,

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.610
did you feel prepared for that role?

00:16:01.610 --> 00:16:03.240
&gt;&gt; No.
And I think that's probably one of

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:07.460
the things that attracted
me Attracted me to it.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:12.400
But I think the other element of it,
is while I've always respected,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:16.180
I've known about Yahoo, I knew about
Yahoo back with it was still Dave's and

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:17.626
Jerry's guide to the worldwide web.

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:21.730
And Jerry Yang used to keep notes
about what the new cool site for

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:24.520
the week was going to
be in his CS directory.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:27.590
So we were always trying
to hack his account and

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:30.150
hope that he had left the permissions
as world readable and

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:33.440
we would all get a little insight into the
new websites that were going to get added

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.520
to the new Yahoo directory that week.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:38.426
And so
I'd known about Yahoo very early on.

00:16:38.426 --> 00:16:42.444
And obviously, at Google, we really,
really looked up to Yahoo,

00:16:42.444 --> 00:16:45.200
ran a Yahoo account in the summer of 2000.

00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:48.670
It was a huge step forward for the
company, it's something we had worked at,

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.740
on, and towards the entire
time I had worked there.

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:54.784
So we'd worked on it for more than
a year when it finally came together.

00:16:54.784 --> 00:16:59.215
And people love Yahoo and I think
that when I look at the service and

00:16:59.215 --> 00:17:04.131
the role this played in the industry,
I think a lot about the products and

00:17:04.131 --> 00:17:09.170
the services and things we all create
as art because they really are.

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:10.410
And a couple of years ago,

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:15.280
I met a friend in the city who has
a little gallery as like a side business.

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:18.620
I said, well, how do you decide
what artists to put in the gallery?

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:21.600
And he said, well I like local artists,
because I like to support local people.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.800
And he's like, but
I also like art from people who are nice.

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:30.420
And he's like, because life is too short
to work with people who aren't nice.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:36.040
And he's like also with an artist you
can feel if they're not nice in the art.

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:37.220
And I thought that was really interesting.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.370
because you feel like when your in art
museum and you look at piece of artwork,

00:17:41.370 --> 00:17:45.185
you can feel when the people
who are kind of tormented or

00:17:45.185 --> 00:17:48.560
they're angry,
you can feel it in their artwork.

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.880
And I thought was actually
really insightful, and

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:53.710
if you think about it, and
women in particular relate to this.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.924
You can tell when the designer of
your clothing likes women, or not.

00:17:56.924 --> 00:17:59.070
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:03.310
Depending on shoes,
other things, you can feel that.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:07.700
Yahoo, and all those years, one I
really respected, what they created and

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:08.650
what they built.

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:12.758
But you could also tell that the people
at Yahoo were nice, and were fun and

00:18:12.758 --> 00:18:15.089
they're hearts were in the right place.

00:18:15.089 --> 00:18:17.921
Even though there were a lot times
when the leadership and the board and

00:18:17.921 --> 00:18:20.514
all those different parts of
the company had their challenges.

00:18:20.514 --> 00:18:25.743
You could tell that when you use
the service, the people there really

00:18:25.743 --> 00:18:30.449
took a lot of inspiration and
joy out of the work that they did.

00:18:30.449 --> 00:18:32.414
And my hope was going to Yahoo,

00:18:32.414 --> 00:18:36.655
that that layer of the company was
still there and it very much was.

00:18:36.655 --> 00:18:38.303
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:18:38.303 --> 00:18:41.683
So building on that point of
the talent within the company,

00:18:41.683 --> 00:18:45.750
you were highly inquisitive in
the earlier years of your time at Yahoo.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:51.560
Some acquisitions were for
technology, others were acqui-hires.

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:56.620
Do you think that that was enough to
transform the company's core mission from

00:18:56.620 --> 00:19:02.400
being media oriented, into a products
company at the leading edge of mobile?

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:05.200
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm proud of
the different things that we did.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.900
And I think that there's a lot
of different elements to

00:19:08.900 --> 00:19:12.215
what is unfolded at Yahoo
in the past few years.

00:19:12.215 --> 00:19:17.020
But the one thing is we had about
a $5 billion base of revenue

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.710
that was declining about 10% per year.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:23.017
And when I had got there at Yahoo, one of
the thing was I like to work hard, and

00:19:23.017 --> 00:19:25.786
I'm a workaholic, and
I like to work on really hard problems.

00:19:25.786 --> 00:19:29.980
When I got there, we sort of looked at
the 10 to 12 different lines of business.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:32.951
And every single one of
them was in decline.

00:19:32.951 --> 00:19:34.917
And when I asked the current leaders for

00:19:34.917 --> 00:19:39.000
the company how are we going to get any
of those growing, nobody had any ideas.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:40.690
The answer was they're going to decline,
they're going to decline, for

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:41.650
the foreseeable future.

00:19:41.650 --> 00:19:45.310
They're going to decline based on
industry trends like, they're declining.

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:49.410
That's a pretty harrowing diagnosis
when you're the new CEO coming

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:50.040
into a turnaround.

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:53.120
It's like, wait,
I've got to get revenue growing, and

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.710
we've got ten business lines
that are all decaying,

00:19:56.710 --> 00:20:01.830
and we're losing about half a million
dollars a year of top line.

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:03.760
And no one has any idea
how to turn it around.

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:06.340
And so, when you're a turnaround CEO,
you've got to make a decision.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:09.970
Can you turn around the existing lines
of business and get them growing again?

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:12.062
Or do you have to invent
new lines of business?

00:20:12.062 --> 00:20:13.920
And so, it became very clear,

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:18.370
within a matter of weeks of arriving,
we need to create new lines of business.

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:21.430
And so for us, that really became
about mobile, not only just products,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:22.660
but mobile.

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:27.470
And the issue is that Yahoo, when I
arrived, had depending on how you counted

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:32.090
it between 30 and 60 mobile
engineers in a company of 14,000.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:36.962
So basically at about $11 million
in mobile revenue in 2011 makes it

00:20:36.962 --> 00:20:38.210
easy to remember.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:43.160
So you're like wait, $11 million
of revenue inside of $5 billion.

00:20:43.160 --> 00:20:46.347
30 people inside of 14,000.

00:20:46.347 --> 00:20:49.098
And I just told the whole board we're
going to turn the company around by

00:20:49.098 --> 00:20:50.590
betting on mobile.

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:54.770
And so we said okay,
we really need to make this move fast.

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:58.560
And the good news is,
mobile was taking over the world, and

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:03.529
there was a real technology, companies
can reinvent themselves in waves.

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:10.230
When you look at IBM rode the wave to
services, Amazon rode the wave to Cloud,

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:11.840
they didn't even really need
to turn themselves around.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:15.210
But these kinds of waves
can really lift companies.

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:17.780
I said look, I think the wave that
we need to catch is mobile, and

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:19.290
we're late catching that wave.

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:21.280
But we need to catch it.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:25.210
So we went about doing different
types of acquisitions.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:28.550
I always thought about acquisitions
in three different buckets.

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:31.380
Small, medium, large, and
the small, medium, and

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:33.375
large each had a different purpose.

00:21:33.375 --> 00:21:37.555
We bought a lot of small companies for
the talent, getting teams in the door and

00:21:37.555 --> 00:21:40.165
getting them in the door quickly enough
because we needed to move fast and

00:21:40.165 --> 00:21:41.895
just start shipping mobile products.

00:21:41.895 --> 00:21:45.385
Getting the mobile apps out there,
start growing our user base,

00:21:45.385 --> 00:21:49.670
which at the time was tens of millions of
users, on mobile today is 650 million.

00:21:49.670 --> 00:21:54.900
Grow that mobile revenue from $11 million
to, last year it was $1.5 billion.

00:21:54.900 --> 00:22:00.740
So if we grew $1.5 billion
of revenue in four years.

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:04.730
And now it looks easy, and
looks like it was the right bet.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:06.610
And the strategy looks like it worked.

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:08.900
But at the time,
there was a lot of debate.

00:22:08.900 --> 00:22:11.290
Should we do HTML5 or
should we do mobile apps?

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:14.540
Does Blackberry matter versus iOS and
Android.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:16.960
To credit to the team,
we made a lot of those decisions,

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:21.262
those strategic decisions right
in the moment when we got there.

00:22:21.262 --> 00:22:22.970
Issues in revenue remained flat,

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:26.540
because we also had this decline that
we were trying to fill in the gap.

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:29.200
So even all that growth
just basically offset

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:33.110
the revenue that was otherwise
leaving the company.

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:35.760
But we did small acquisitions for teams.

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:40.390
We did medium acquisitions for
technology and teams basically.

00:22:40.390 --> 00:22:43.410
For example, like a good,
I call them building blocks.

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:46.800
We bought a company called Zombie that was
really good at organizing contacts and

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:47.610
analyzing your email.

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:52.600
And we bought that to become a new
foundational block for Yahoo Mail,

00:22:52.600 --> 00:22:54.440
which is our biggest product.

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:58.340
And then we also took some big
strategic bets, like Tumblr and

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:03.640
BrightRoll, because I really feel that
if you look at technology companies,

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.630
they reinvent themselves
through acquisitions.

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:09.600
Sometimes a new product idea comes
from within, like Amazon Web Services.

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:13.080
A lot of times if you look at Google,
how transformational Android and

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:14.910
YouTube were for them.

00:23:14.910 --> 00:23:18.180
Facebook, how transformational
Instagram and Whatsapp are.

00:23:19.242 --> 00:23:23.230
You've gotta make some big bets and
those can really help reinvent.

00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:28.260
And so, I felt that we needed to
do everything that we could given

00:23:28.260 --> 00:23:33.320
some of the size of the task to both work
to invent new business lines inside.

00:23:33.320 --> 00:23:34.470
But also try and

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:40.100
inject new talent and new strategic ideas
that could help without reinvention.

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:43.860
&gt;&gt; Despite the fact that that strategy
seems to have played out fairly well.

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:49.070
You remain as CEO, as a company,
you remain under a lot of public scrutiny.

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:53.450
That's perhaps never been the case more so

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:57.450
than today,
given that privacy concerns and security.

00:23:59.190 --> 00:24:05.550
So given that, as a leader,
how do you rise above the criticism and

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:07.815
And as a person,
how do you not take that heart?

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.577
&gt;&gt; A couple of things,
I just don't read it, and

00:24:12.577 --> 00:24:13.993
that might sound-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:24:13.993 --> 00:24:16.859
&gt;&gt; That might sound tone deaf.

00:24:16.859 --> 00:24:21.525
But I feel like if you read it,
it changes who you are and

00:24:21.525 --> 00:24:24.410
how you think about things.

00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:28.000
If something's, if one of your decisions
gets reinforced as being super,

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:31.070
super smart, but it's actually wrong, and

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:34.080
you read that this was
the smartest decision ever,

00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:38.930
reading that's going to make you less
likely to abandon it or change course.

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:42.699
If you made the best decision ever and

00:24:42.699 --> 00:24:46.954
you read that no, it's probably wrong,

00:24:46.954 --> 00:24:51.831
it's going to make you
more likely to abandon it.

00:24:51.831 --> 00:24:55.931
And the truth is, the press is the press.

00:24:55.931 --> 00:24:56.936
There are some times when they're smart,
and

00:24:56.936 --> 00:24:58.340
there are some times when they're right.

00:24:58.340 --> 00:25:00.290
But they don't have half
of the information,

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:03.970
even close to half of the information
that you have being in the company,

00:25:03.970 --> 00:25:06.850
in terms of what's likely to play out and
what's not.

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:10.510
And so to make decisions or allow
your decision-making to be influenced

00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:16.170
by a third party who has a fraction
of the information that you

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:19.902
have, just overall isn't
a great strategy to me.

00:25:19.902 --> 00:25:21.910
That said,
I try not to be deaf to it, right.

00:25:21.910 --> 00:25:25.530
I've got a PR team that will summarize
some of the bigger issues and

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:27.290
points that are being made.

00:25:27.290 --> 00:25:29.400
I also have a husband.

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:32.830
If I'm worried enough about something,
I'll ask him like, hey [LAUGH].

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.610
I am on Twitter, I read headlines.

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:37.900
I tend not to click on them, but I'll be
like, I'm seeing this headline a lot.

00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:40.315
What's going on with that?

00:25:40.315 --> 00:25:41.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I do read other things.

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:43.950
I just don't tend to read
things about Yahoo or myself.

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:47.877
I try and have those, because I also think
that if you're going to get criticism,

00:25:47.877 --> 00:25:50.913
it always sounds better coming
from someone who loves you.

00:25:50.913 --> 00:25:51.928
&gt;&gt; I would agree.
&gt;&gt; Or someone that you work with and

00:25:51.928 --> 00:25:52.450
have a relationship with.

00:25:52.450 --> 00:25:55.720
I remember there was,
less of a scholar and pundit.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:59.677
But I remember I saw an interview
with Bradley Cooper, the actor,

00:25:59.677 --> 00:26:01.666
on the Late Night talk show once.

00:26:01.666 --> 00:26:02.915
And he said,
do you know what I've discovered?

00:26:02.915 --> 00:26:05.390
I discovered if you ever
wanted to feel really,

00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:08.206
really bad about yourself,
go read about yourself on the Internet.

00:26:08.206 --> 00:26:10.571
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So it works if you're an actor, or

00:26:10.571 --> 00:26:11.670
it works if you're a business leader.

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:13.829
Or, by the way,
it works if you're a high school student.

00:26:13.829 --> 00:26:15.062
All right, it is a problem.

00:26:15.062 --> 00:26:19.740
But the point is,
you just can't let that shape who you are.

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:22.770
And so
you either have to have guidelines for

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:25.900
how you're going to absorb it, like I do.

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:27.560
Or you have to have very thick skin.

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:30.450
I tend to think I have
a little bit of both.

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:34.663
But you have to have a way of
cutting through all that noise,

00:26:34.663 --> 00:26:39.650
to what's a valid criticism that
needs to be acted on and what isn't?

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:41.610
&gt;&gt; We have a motto here at the GSB.

00:26:41.610 --> 00:26:44.421
It's somewhat tongue-in-cheek,
that feedback is a gift, and

00:26:44.421 --> 00:26:45.432
all feedback is a gift.

00:26:45.432 --> 00:26:47.695
&gt;&gt; Well, I think, yeah, on the flip side,

00:26:47.695 --> 00:26:51.406
one of my favorite colleagues at
Google is a guy named Alan Eustace.

00:26:51.406 --> 00:26:54.035
And he rolled this thing
in a one-on-one once.

00:26:54.035 --> 00:26:58.135
Where he just said, Marissa,
the one reason that criticism

00:26:58.135 --> 00:27:00.765
hurts is because it's almost
always some percent true.

00:27:01.910 --> 00:27:05.101
Right, the question is,
is it 1% true or is it 75% true?

00:27:05.101 --> 00:27:06.607
Or is it 100% true, right?

00:27:06.607 --> 00:27:09.660
But he's like, that's why criticism
strikes a nerve with people.

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:14.293
Because there's almost
always some truth to it.

00:27:14.293 --> 00:27:16.020
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.
In light of that, how did you think about

00:27:17.200 --> 00:27:21.730
absorbing the feedback from Yahoo
employees and from those outside of

00:27:21.730 --> 00:27:26.110
the company about both your own personal
decision to return to work shortly after

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:30.540
the birth of your three children,
as well as your policies regarding

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:34.920
employees working remotely, and the family
leave policies that you put in place.

00:27:36.220 --> 00:27:38.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think that that's probably, I mean,

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:41.090
there's been a lot of things that have
been misunderstood about my time at Yahoo.

00:27:41.090 --> 00:27:44.350
I think that my thoughts
on all of those things and

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:47.570
how they actually played out
are probably the most misunderstood.

00:27:49.770 --> 00:27:52.650
A couple of things,
I have nothing against maternity leave.

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:55.440
Everyone should take them, if you can.

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:59.990
But I will say the company was just not in
the state where I could take a maternity

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:03.500
leave in either time that it came time for
me to have my children.

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:08.520
When I was at Google, Google has
glorious maternity leave policies.

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:10.750
I had been there for 12 years.

00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:15.240
I could be, between a combination
of time that I had stored

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:19.060
up as well as maternity leave,
my plan was to take a six-month leave.

00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:21.650
All right, and Google wasn't even
going to turn off my stock vesting.

00:28:21.650 --> 00:28:25.300
I was going to have one of the best
compensated maternity leaves of all time

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:26.300
at Google.

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:29.510
And then this opportunity
at Yahoo presented itself.

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:30.540
I remember thinking wow,

00:28:30.540 --> 00:28:35.190
if I take this, two months after I
get there I'm going to have a baby.

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:36.720
I can't be out for six months.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:41.080
I can't go and be CEO for two months and
then be gone for three, four, six months.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.993
And that just also wasn't fair to Yahoo,
given the state that it was in, and

00:28:44.993 --> 00:28:48.860
how much time was really of the essence
to catch up in the mobile race, etc.

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:50.507
And so I thought to myself,

00:28:50.507 --> 00:28:53.810
I just am going to to have to
do this in a different way.

00:28:53.810 --> 00:28:56.340
I'm going to have to push to
come back to work sooner.

00:28:56.340 --> 00:29:00.912
It doesn't mean that I don't think it's
reasonable to have maternity leave or

00:29:00.912 --> 00:29:05.020
would advocate for people giving up
their maternity the way that I did.

00:29:05.020 --> 00:29:06.410
But this was an opportunity.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:09.810
I had an obligation to my employees,
to my shareholders.

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:14.200
And I also felt that time
was incredibly important.

00:29:14.200 --> 00:29:18.296
At the same time, I also said to myself
okay, I've gotta come up with a way to

00:29:18.296 --> 00:29:22.170
make sure that I can also be the best
mother that I can be, right.

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:25.190
And so what we basically came
up with is I was out for

00:29:25.190 --> 00:29:29.450
eight to ten business days, and
I then was back in the office.

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:32.900
But, and a lot has been made of it.

00:29:32.900 --> 00:29:38.190
It has been called a nursery, it's
actually a small closet off my office.

00:29:38.190 --> 00:29:41.420
But I would bring my son
in to the office with me.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:44.130
So we joked that the first day he came to
the office he had this little Ralph Lauren

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:46.330
navy and white little suit thing.

00:29:46.330 --> 00:29:48.677
It was more comfy, it was kind of knit.

00:29:48.677 --> 00:29:53.751
But it was like he had his office wear,
he was like the business baby [LAUGH].

00:29:53.751 --> 00:29:57.850
And so he came in and
he was there in the office with me.

00:29:57.850 --> 00:30:00.640
And I was there doing the job,
being in meetings.

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:05.033
And every four or five hours I would
go in, see him, do a conference call.

00:30:05.033 --> 00:30:06.770
It turns out kids,
when they're learning to talk,

00:30:06.770 --> 00:30:08.880
they just need to hear a lot of language.

00:30:08.880 --> 00:30:10.612
And so I would just be on speaker phone.

00:30:10.612 --> 00:30:16.500
So my son understands revenue and EBITDA,
and everything really well [LAUGH].

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:20.164
And so that's sort of how we worked it.

00:30:20.164 --> 00:30:22.830
And it wasn't ideal, but
I had a lot of time with him.

00:30:22.830 --> 00:30:25.410
I think I had a lot more time with
him than most people recognize.

00:30:25.410 --> 00:30:28.630
And I almost ended up doing
my maternity leave inverted.

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:31.540
Right, some people are home for their
maternity leave, and calling into work.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:38.270
I happened to be at work with my son
there, but it ultimately worked.

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:40.390
It gave me the time that
I needed to be CEO,

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:44.000
and also the time that I needed with him.

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:48.400
And then when we went to revise
our family leave policies,

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:52.190
because Yahoo previously didn't have
very generous family leave policies.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:30:58.090
We said, look, I really believe in
taking it from the child's viewpoint.

00:30:58.090 --> 00:31:00.940
And I said, look, because there's so
many different ways to have children now.

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:03.168
There's biological mothers,
there's adoption,

00:31:03.168 --> 00:31:04.808
there's the atraditional families.

00:31:04.808 --> 00:31:08.404
I said, okay, flip it around
from the child's perspective.

00:31:08.404 --> 00:31:10.336
Any child of a Yahoo should have,

00:31:10.336 --> 00:31:14.760
let's call it at least two months
at home with their Yahoo parent.

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:16.780
Be it mom, be it dad, etc.

00:31:16.780 --> 00:31:19.880
And then biological moms of course,
also gets state afforded leave.

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:22.440
And so what we do now is any parent,

00:31:22.440 --> 00:31:28.280
any new parent gets eight weeks in
the first year of the child's life.

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:30.490
And biological mothers can
get up to 16 weeks, so

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:34.490
basically four months of time off.

00:31:34.490 --> 00:31:38.100
But that was something that
was overall important to me.

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:42.792
Because that time, whether you're in my
case where I was spending it in the office

00:31:42.792 --> 00:31:47.090
or you're spending it at home with
your child, is incredibly important.

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:49.578
And for the work from home,
I may be eventually,

00:31:49.578 --> 00:31:52.990
I'm just always the poster child for
being anti-work from home.

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:54.980
I actually have nothing
against work from home.

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:58.070
My brother works from home,
lots of people work from home.

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:01.430
And I think it's a perfectly
valid way to do things,

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:04.947
but One of the things that I experienced
when I first got to Yahoo is,

00:32:04.947 --> 00:32:07.699
the company had been through so
much turbulence, and energy was low.

00:32:07.699 --> 00:32:11.891
And one of the challenges I had was,
I had to re-energize the campus.

00:32:11.891 --> 00:32:15.985
And it was just really hard, because
it was us and people who had dedicated

00:32:15.985 --> 00:32:19.617
setups, where they were working
from home 100% of the time,

00:32:19.617 --> 00:32:23.974
but it was the person who said, you know
what it's raining really hard today,

00:32:23.974 --> 00:32:26.641
I think I'm going to work
from home on Wednesday.

00:32:26.641 --> 00:32:29.654
And then we'd have high performing Yahoos,
coming and talking to me and saying,

00:32:29.654 --> 00:32:32.798
hey you're here, we're building all this
stuff, we're doing all this stuff, and

00:32:32.798 --> 00:32:35.767
it's really frustrating that every now and
then, I got this person on my team who

00:32:35.767 --> 00:32:38.433
just doesn't show up for work, and
tells they're working from home but

00:32:38.433 --> 00:32:41.380
it doesn't really feel like
they're working from home.

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:42.590
Right.
because I know, I mean to me,

00:32:42.590 --> 00:32:45.570
when you got a dedicated setup,
you're probably really productive.

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:48.760
But I know, if I didn't have
a dedicated setup at home,

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:52.410
I'm just sitting there at the kitchen
counter, or on the couch doing email.

00:32:52.410 --> 00:32:55.680
I'm productive, but I'm not as productive
as I am when I'm in the office, and

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.450
meeting with people and
interacting with people.

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:00.820
And so we said look,
work from home is fine.

00:33:00.820 --> 00:33:04.376
This is not statement from, as it was
ultimately misconstrued in the media,

00:33:04.376 --> 00:33:07.726
this is not a statement about whether or
not work from home is good or bad.

00:33:07.726 --> 00:33:09.760
But this is Yahoo's moment.

00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:10.740
We're an iconic company.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:12.450
We're trying to return it to greatness.

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:13.990
We need all hands on deck.

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:16.890
And so the statement that we
ultimately released said look,

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:19.750
this is not even a permanent policy for
the company.

00:33:19.750 --> 00:33:23.738
It's just that, for right now, for us
the right thing is, to have everybody who

00:33:23.738 --> 00:33:27.552
isn't formally working from home,
because we do have about 100 people in

00:33:27.552 --> 00:33:31.366
the company who do formally work from
home, you should be here in the office,

00:33:31.366 --> 00:33:34.858
because this is our moment,
this is our time, and we need to seize it.

00:33:34.858 --> 00:33:36.850
And so, that's really what that was about.

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:41.650
And ironically, it was unfortunately
characterized, as kind of coming raining

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:46.680
down on working moms, or
conflated with my maternity leave.

00:33:46.680 --> 00:33:49.280
In truth, about 85% of the people
affected by that change were men.

00:33:51.430 --> 00:33:54.390
And in total, there was about a hundred
people a day who work from home,

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:57.790
and there were about a hundred people or
so, where their status changed, and

00:33:57.790 --> 00:33:58.990
that only 15 of them were women.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:04.590
And you never know that from the thousands
of articles that were written.

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:05.280
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:34:05.280 --> 00:34:09.238
We were talking behind
stage about your husband,

00:34:09.238 --> 00:34:12.639
who is a venture-capitalist in the area.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:16.974
You're from Wisconsin.

00:34:16.974 --> 00:34:21.054
In thinking about raising
a family in Silicon Valley, and

00:34:21.054 --> 00:34:24.800
in the spot light,
what concerns might you have for,

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:30.150
making sure that your children have
the same sort of childhood that you had?

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:32.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:33.070
So, I have three children.

00:34:33.070 --> 00:34:37.477
I've got a four year old boy, and
identical twin girls who are one.

00:34:37.477 --> 00:34:40.346
And no, they're amazing.

00:34:40.346 --> 00:34:42.781
I would say, I wasn't sure,
I know this sounds funny, but

00:34:42.781 --> 00:34:44.850
I wasn't sure about motherhood.

00:34:44.850 --> 00:34:47.320
My husband was very gung ho,
we're having kids.

00:34:47.320 --> 00:34:52.335
And I was like, okay.
[LAUGH].

00:34:52.335 --> 00:34:58.020
And I have to say,
it is really just amazing overall.

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:02.092
I knew I loved kids, but
to me as I said, I had my APMs,

00:35:02.092 --> 00:35:06.729
I love other people's kids,
do I need to have some of my own?

00:35:06.729 --> 00:35:08.500
I mean it's really amazing.

00:35:08.500 --> 00:35:11.390
I would encourage it for
absolutely everyone.

00:35:11.390 --> 00:35:13.900
because it just gives you so
much perspective.

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:20.730
And you just realize that some of the
biggest impacts you can have in the world,

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:27.059
is raising those kids to be,
productive, creative, amazing people.

00:35:27.059 --> 00:35:33.619
But to me, really I think it's
the timing and place is different.

00:35:33.619 --> 00:35:38.307
That said, the values you provide
your children are the same,

00:35:38.307 --> 00:35:42.473
that perspective,
to me ultimately remains the same.

00:35:42.473 --> 00:35:46.937
And there's certainly challenges but, I
think that for Macallister who's a little

00:35:46.937 --> 00:35:50.100
bit older, I want him to have
an overstimulated childhood.

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:52.712
I want him to be exposed to everything.

00:35:52.712 --> 00:35:57.670
Art, science, I mean Palo Alto and
the Bay Area is just so amazing.

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:02.017
You've got retired Stanford
professors teaching science classes,

00:36:02.017 --> 00:36:06.150
at the science museum in downtown
Palo Alto, which is unheard of.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:10.270
There's bilingual Mandarin story time
around the block from our house.

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:13.210
Right, I mean it's really,
really an amazing gift to be

00:36:13.210 --> 00:36:17.330
able to raise kids here, and
be able to expose them to so much.

00:36:17.330 --> 00:36:19.400
And so,
helping them get expose to that, but

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:22.354
also having them realize that
the whole world isn't like that.

00:36:22.354 --> 00:36:26.508
And my husband's from Denver,
I'm from Wisconsin, and

00:36:26.508 --> 00:36:31.016
you go home and see the way
different parts of the country work.

00:36:31.016 --> 00:36:36.650
And feeling that is important, and even
more so different places in the world.

00:36:36.650 --> 00:36:38.227
So we tend to travel a lot, and

00:36:38.227 --> 00:36:41.968
we want to make sure that we expose
our kids to a lot of different things.

00:36:41.968 --> 00:36:46.815
But, interestingly I had a Stanford
professor, Terry Winograd,

00:36:46.815 --> 00:36:50.309
who raised two absolutely
terrific daughters.

00:36:50.309 --> 00:36:52.592
And I really admired
the way he raised his kids.

00:36:52.592 --> 00:36:55.842
And, I'm friends with his wife, and

00:36:55.842 --> 00:37:00.624
I asked her one time,
I said your girls are so terrific.

00:37:00.624 --> 00:37:01.520
What did you do?

00:37:01.520 --> 00:37:03.970
And she said one,
travel is really important.

00:37:03.970 --> 00:37:07.300
Them understanding,
Silicon Valley's context in the world,

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:11.640
and how lots of different people live and
work is really important.

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:14.100
But she also said the other thing is,

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:16.880
a lot of people worry about all
the success of Silicon Valley and

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:21.030
all that, and the impact it will have,
and I think that weighs on everybody.

00:37:21.030 --> 00:37:25.335
But, she also said the thing
you have to watch out for,

00:37:25.335 --> 00:37:28.425
because of the proximity to Stanford,

00:37:28.425 --> 00:37:33.384
is that you really need to make
sure that they understand that,

00:37:33.384 --> 00:37:38.183
it's an issue of academic and
intellectual self-esteem.

00:37:38.183 --> 00:37:41.347
But everybody here is so smart.

00:37:41.347 --> 00:37:43.990
And when they're going to
the Palo Alto public schools, right?

00:37:43.990 --> 00:37:46.610
They're going with the children
of brilliant engineers, and

00:37:46.610 --> 00:37:49.290
brilliant professors,
and Stanford students.

00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:54.620
And that, ultimately, yeah, there are some
things, they don't necessarily understand

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:58.710
how smart, how talented they are, because
of the context in which they grew up.

00:37:58.710 --> 00:38:02.031
So making sure that your kids are really
confident, that they got good self-esteem,

00:38:02.031 --> 00:38:03.623
that they know what they're good at, and

00:38:03.623 --> 00:38:06.409
that you're really fostering is
something that's really important.

00:38:06.409 --> 00:38:07.160
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:09.383
And you feel that's possible,

00:38:09.383 --> 00:38:13.263
even within the context of
them being in the spotlight?

00:38:13.263 --> 00:38:18.210
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, I think that my view is,
I'm in the spotlight because of my role.

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:19.440
They aren't, right?

00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:26.210
And I'm very protective of their routines,
their days.

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:30.383
You can scrutinize me.

00:38:30.383 --> 00:38:32.280
Through my roles, I'm out there that way.

00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:33.800
But that's not really fair to them,

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:36.890
and I think that overall there
hasn't been a big spotlight on them.

00:38:36.890 --> 00:38:39.736
I think that their names are known, but

00:38:39.736 --> 00:38:43.300
we're pretty careful
with what they look like.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:44.747
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Their identities, where they go,

00:38:44.747 --> 00:38:45.522
all that type of thing.

00:38:45.522 --> 00:38:46.802
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:38:46.802 --> 00:38:48.650
So, we have time for
one more question for me, and

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:50.280
then we'll open up to the audience.

00:38:50.280 --> 00:38:54.568
But, fast forward a few months from now,
the Verizon acquisition closes.

00:38:54.568 --> 00:38:56.890
What do you wake up, excited to do next?

00:38:57.950 --> 00:38:59.110
Is it at Yahoo?

00:38:59.110 --> 00:39:03.014
Is it outside of Yahoo,
within the tech community?

00:39:03.014 --> 00:39:07.142
&gt;&gt; I mean, I think that right
now it's too early to say.

00:39:07.142 --> 00:39:11.227
I mean we were working, overall in
closing the Verizon transaction.

00:39:11.227 --> 00:39:14.641
And that's the most important thing
that I'm working on, right now.

00:39:14.641 --> 00:39:18.015
But, given that reflection that I had last

00:39:18.015 --> 00:39:21.400
summer of what am I most
proud of at Google?

00:39:21.400 --> 00:39:22.615
And what am I most proud of at Yahoo?

00:39:22.615 --> 00:39:28.070
It's really that it's rare privilege,
to work a company that changes the world.

00:39:28.070 --> 00:39:31.023
Because there's lots of great
companies out there, but

00:39:31.023 --> 00:39:34.848
there's a rare few that really change
the world, and change paradigms.

00:39:34.848 --> 00:39:37.538
And I'm excited to work on that.

00:39:37.538 --> 00:39:43.830
Whether that's at Verizon, or at Yahoo,
or somewhere else, but it's addictive.

00:39:43.830 --> 00:39:47.870
That ability to, really try and
help change the world,

00:39:47.870 --> 00:39:50.720
make an impact on the world for
the better.

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:54.241
And the fact that, we now have so many
tools that are available to our reach,

00:39:54.241 --> 00:39:57.378
to really influence those kinds of
outcomes, is pretty amazing and

00:39:57.378 --> 00:39:58.935
is an opportunity not to be lost.

00:39:58.935 --> 00:40:01.319
&gt;&gt; I think I speak for
everyone in the audience when I say that,

00:40:01.319 --> 00:40:04.055
I hope your future plans include,
teaching back here at Stanford.

00:40:04.055 --> 00:40:11.309
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:40:11.309 --> 00:40:16.685
